---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="P-DARTS Skin Transferability | Amirhossein Eskorouchi"
  description="Architecture transferability with Progressive Differentiable Architecture Search for skin-lesion diagnosis."
>
  <article class="project-page">
    <header class="project-header">
      <p class="project-kicker">
        Medical AI &middot; Neural Architecture Search &middot; Transferability
      </p>

      <h1>
        Understanding Knowledge Transferability in Differentiable Architecture
        Search for Skin Lesion Diagnosis
      </h1>

      <p class="project-authors">
        Anh Vo, Lu He,{" "}<strong>Amirhossein Eskorouchi</strong>, and Haifeng Wang
      </p>

      <p class="project-venue">
        <em>IISE Transactions on Healthcare Systems Engineering</em>,
        16(1), 71-90, 2026
      </p>

      <p class="project-links">
        <a
          href="https://doi.org/10.1080/24725579.2026.2622416"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noreferrer"
        >
          Article
        </a>
        <span aria-hidden="true"> / </span>
        <a
          href="https://github.com/amirhossein-eskorouchi/pdarts-skin-transferability"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noreferrer"
        >
          Code
        </a>
        <span aria-hidden="true"> / </span>
        <a
          href="https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/24725579.2026.2622416"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noreferrer"
        >
          Publisher page
        </a>
      </p>
    </header>

    <figure class="project-hero-figure">
      <img
        src="/images/projects/pdarts-skin-transferability/architecture-transfer-workflow.png"
        alt="Experimental workflow for transferring a searched neural architecture from a source skin-lesion dataset to a target dataset with new target-task weights"
      />
      <figcaption>
        The study transfers the discovered architecture, not the source-trained
        weights. Each target model is initialized and trained anew.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section class="project-detail-section">
      <h2>Research question</h2>

      <p>
        Neural architecture search can identify task-specific network
        structures, but repeating the search for every new medical-imaging
        dataset is computationally expensive. This work asks whether a cell
        architecture discovered on a larger dermoscopic source dataset can be
        reused effectively when the target images differ in acquisition
        modality, sample size, class balance, and resolution.
      </p>

      <p>
        The study isolates architectural knowledge from pretrained feature
        transfer. Progressive Differentiable Architecture Search discovers
        normal and reduction cells on a source dataset. Those cells are then
        stacked into target evaluation networks, assigned newly initialized
        weights, and trained on the target data from scratch.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="project-detail-section">
      <h2>Study design</h2>

      <div class="project-fact-grid">
        <div>
          <h3>Source and targets</h3>
          <p>
            ISIC-2019 serves as the primary search source. PAD-UFES-20 and
            DermaMNIST provide cross-dataset targets with different image
            characteristics and data regimes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3>Architecture depth</h3>
          <p>
            P-DARTS searches at depths 6, 8, and 10. The discovered cells are
            evaluated in networks containing 2 through 14 cells.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3>Evaluation settings</h3>
          <p>
            The experiments cover six-class and malignant-versus-benign
            diagnosis at 32&times;32 and 224&times;224 resolutions.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3>Validation</h3>
          <p>
            Performance is examined using accuracy, weighted F1, sensitivity,
            specificity, AUC, repeated architectures, and statistical
            comparisons.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="project-detail-section">
      <h2>Key findings</h2>

      <ul>
        <li>
          Transferred P-DARTS architectures remained competitive across
          PAD-UFES-20 and DermaMNIST despite changes in acquisition conditions
          and dataset scale.
        </li>

        <li>
          At 224&times;224 resolution, the best reported mean accuracies were
          69.06% on PAD-UFES-20 and 79.48% on DermaMNIST.
        </li>

        <li>
          The best reported individual PAD-UFES-20 result reached 63.70% for
          six-class diagnosis and 97.71% for malignant-versus-benign
          classification.
        </li>

        <li>
          Evaluation-network depth was more influential than the absolute gap
          between search depth and evaluation depth.
        </li>

        <li>
          Pooling operations in the discovered reduction cells were associated
          with stronger transfer behavior.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="project-detail-section">
      <h2>Architecture and transfer behavior</h2>

      <div class="project-media-grid balanced">
        <figure>
          <img
            src="/images/projects/pdarts-skin-transferability/source-target-performance.png"
            alt="Test accuracy of P-DARTS and DARTS architectures across source and target datasets as evaluation depth changes"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption>
            Source and target accuracy across evaluation depths for P-DARTS6,
            P-DARTS8, P-DARTS10, and DARTS6.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <figure>
          <img
            src="/images/projects/pdarts-skin-transferability/pdarts6-discovered-cells.png"
            alt="Normal and reduction cells discovered by P-DARTS6 for the skin-lesion architecture-transfer study"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption>
            A representative P-DARTS6 normal cell and reduction cell
            transferred into target evaluation networks.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="project-detail-section">
      <h2>Research contribution</h2>

      <p>
        The project provides a controlled study of transfer at the architecture
        level. It separates reusable network structure from pretrained weights
        and examines how source-data properties, search depth, evaluation
        depth, cell operations, and image resolution shape transfer
        performance. This offers evidence that architecture search can produce
        reusable structural knowledge for data-limited medical-imaging tasks.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="project-detail-section project-note">
      <h2>Responsible interpretation</h2>

      <p>
        This is a retrospective research study, not a clinical diagnostic
        system. Historical PAD-UFES-20 experiments used image-level splits with
        possible patient overlap, so future patient-grouped validation must be
        reported separately. The repository preserves publication-reported
        results and reproducibility records but does not claim that the
        maintained package has regenerated the entire manuscript experiment
        grid.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="project-detail-section">
      <h2>Citation</h2>

      <p>
        Vo, A., He, L., Eskorouchi, A., and Wang, H. (2026). Understanding
        knowledge transferability in differentiable architecture search for
        skin lesion diagnosis.
        <em>IISE Transactions on Healthcare Systems Engineering</em>,
        16(1), 71-90.
      </p>

      <p>
        <a
          href="https://doi.org/10.1080/24725579.2026.2622416"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noreferrer"
        >
          https://doi.org/10.1080/24725579.2026.2622416
        </a>
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .project-page {
    width: 100%;
  }

  .project-header {
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }

  .project-kicker {
    color: var(--muted, #536174);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .project-header h1 {
    max-width: 58rem;
  }

  .project-authors,
  .project-venue,
  .project-links {
    margin: 0.55rem 0;
  }

  .project-hero-figure img,
  .project-media-grid img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }

  .project-hero-figure {
    max-width: 64rem;
    margin: 2rem auto 3rem;
  }

  figcaption {
    margin-top: 0.65rem;
    color: var(--muted, #536174);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .project-detail-section {
    margin-top: 3rem;
  }

  .project-detail-section > p,
  .project-detail-section > ul {
    max-width: 70rem;
  }

  .project-detail-section li + li {
    margin-top: 0.65rem;
  }

  .project-fact-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 1.5rem 2rem;
  }

  .project-fact-grid > div {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border, #dfe5ec);
    padding-top: 1rem;
  }

  .project-fact-grid h3 {
    margin: 0 0 0.45rem;
  }

  .project-fact-grid p {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .project-media-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 2rem;
    align-items: start;
  }

  .project-media-grid figure {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .project-note {
    border-left: 3px solid var(--border, #dfe5ec);
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
  }

  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .project-fact-grid,
    .project-media-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }

    .project-detail-section {
      margin-top: 2.25rem;
    }
  }
</style>